ef 17592186499550 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499440 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499551 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499440 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding a Value by Approximating Ratios" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499440 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186499464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499440 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "a02676df-7c77-5303-9d8e-7c83fdfd2060" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499441 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499441 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499441 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499441 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499441 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499441 #edb/ref 1039 "Approximating Ratios by Rounding to the Nearest Hundred" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499441 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186499450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499441 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c71ff81f-388d-53c8-853e-c60430327148" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499581 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186499582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499581 #edb/ref 1061 648009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499581 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3990eff7-9439-5ea7-a839-81c63a117a93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499581 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186499434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499582 #edb/ref 1061 648012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499582 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "39d2b0a2-a796-5c2d-87be-ef9b250b51be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499582 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186499440 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499591 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186499607 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499591 #edb/ref 1061 648005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499591 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "64bb8dc3-c0c9-5342-9899-e92b00265817" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499591 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186499441 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499598 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186499610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499598 #edb/ref 1061 648000 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499598 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "805b138d-9de3-5c18-96bd-507752cfcb3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499598 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186499639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499607 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186499581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499607 #edb/ref 1061 648008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499607 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9d54fdb2-7a7b-5fda-81dd-61e03fb3fa56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499607 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186499634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499610 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186499591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499610 #edb/ref 1061 648001 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499610 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "aa6626df-e1ca-57ff-8e3c-685699ee9c0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499610 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186499435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499634 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, let's see how we can find a value given the ratio and another value.\n\nSuppose at a school library, there are some science books and $396$ history books. The ratio of the number of science books to the number of history books is $3: 4.$ By rounding the number of history books to the nearest ten, approximate the number of science books in the library.\n\nFirst, let's approximate the number of history books:\n\n$$\n396 \\longrightarrow 400\n$$\n\nThe ratio of the number of science books to the number of history books is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{\\color{red}4}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nHere, \n\n- the numerator represents the number of science books, and\n\n- the denominator represents the number of history books.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $400$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $100{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 \\times 100}{4 \\times 100} = \\dfrac{300}{400}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are approximately $300$ science books in the library." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499634 #edb/ref 1065 14923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499634 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "16e103a1-de00-5acb-9566-24168dec17eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499634 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding a Value Given a Ratio" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499639 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use what we know about rounding numbers to approximate ratios.\nWe round the quantities to a given degree first, before setting up the ratio.\n\nLook at the following table, which shows the number of girls in a ballet school with blonde or black hair.\n\n| Blonde Hair | Black Hair |\n| --- | --- |\n| $33$ | $58$ |\n\nRounding to the nearest ten, let's determine the approximate ratio of girls with blonde hair to girls with black hair.\n\nFirst, let's approximate the number of girls with blonde or black hair by rounding to the nearest ten.\n\n- Number of girls with blonde hair: $33 \\longrightarrow 30$\n\n- Number of girls with black hair: $58 \\longrightarrow 60$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of girls with blonde hair to the number of girls with black hair is approximately\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30}{60}.\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing both numbers by $10$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{30 \\div 10}{60 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{3}{6}\n$$\n\nThen, we divide by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3 \\div 3}{6 \\div 3} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the number of girls with blonde hair to the number of girls with black hair is approximately $\\dfrac{1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499639 #edb/ref 1065 2052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499639 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "777fe794-e3cc-5250-a9e8-e407cc3f306c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499639 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
